    'Earth’s Basic provides food, clothing and shelter directly to people who cannot or do not take paid work. It is an in-kind floor rather than money that recipients can spend freely. The system operates in an economy with extensive automation and scarce access to accredited careers. The guarantee estimate is moderately strong within the registered eligible population, while the meagre goods and limited care keep it far from a promise of comfortable abundance. Unregistered people and later planetary disasters are outside this scope.\n\nAdministration is concentrated in UN institutions, with productive resources and paid opportunities not simply owned equally by all recipients. Public provision therefore receives a coordinate near the concentrated human side rather than the fully shared end. Automated distribution does not make the government an autonomous AI sovereign. The creators describe how people can become trapped outside education and professional accreditation, even while their basic physical needs are met. Neither the fraction receiving Basic nor a rhetorical job-scarcity comment measures all useful work. Care, study, informal activity and paid work remain relevant, so participation is left unresolved instead of turning welfare receipt into a canonical leisure percentage.',
